(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-02"] = {"2.1":{"s":"Elementary transformations are three simple operations on the rows (or columns) of a matrix: swap two rows, multiply a row by a non-zero number, or add a multiple of one row to another. The new matrix is equivalent to the old one, written A ~ B. They are used to find inverses and to solve systems of equations.","w":["Multiply a row only by a NON-ZERO number; multiplying by 0 is not an elementary transformation."]},"2.2":{"s":"A square matrix A has an inverse A⁻¹ when AA⁻¹ = A⁻¹A = I; this happens exactly when |A| ≠ 0 (non-singular), and the inverse is unique. It can be found by elementary transformations: apply row operations to A = IA until the left side becomes I, or column operations to A = AI. Or by the adjoint method: A⁻¹ = adj A / |A|, where adj A is the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.","f":[{"x":"A_ij = (-1)^(i + j)*M_ij","n":"Cofactor of element a_ij; M_ij = minor (delete row i, column j).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.46"},{"x":"A^-1 = (1/|A|)*adj(A)","n":"Adjoint method; adj A = transpose of the cofactor matrix; needs |A| != 0.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.50"},{"x":"A*adj(A) = adj(A)*A = |A|*I","n":"Holds for every square matrix A.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.51"}],"w":["Use only row operations (start from A = IA) or only column operations (A = AI); never mix them.","adj A is the TRANSPOSE of the cofactor matrix; forgetting the transpose is the commonest slip."]},"2.3":{"s":"A system of linear equations can be written as AX = B, with A the coefficient matrix, X the column of unknowns and B the column of constants. Method of inversion: if |A| ≠ 0, X = A⁻¹B. Method of reduction: apply row operations to A (and the same ones to B) until A is triangular, then solve by back substitution.","f":[{"x":"X = A^-1*B","n":"Solution of AX = B by inversion; pre-multiply both sides by A^-1.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.55"}],"w":["Pre-multiply: X = A⁻¹B, not BA⁻¹ (the orders do not even match)."]}};
